title The lap that was already green, and the number that moved while its stamp held
prompt The baton -- open the round, read the rota row, take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, reds first.
rota lap 4055, N mod 5 = 0 -- Aether, the row that hears. Read the threshold whole and the cardinal seat whole. This tree read rows 1, 2 and 3 today, so row 0 stands fresh and no hand advance was needed. Its sense is sound, and its fixed pair is sound/silence -- the work speaking against the work holding. That is this lap in one line: a guard made a sound and the box was silent, so the lap answered the sound rather than starting something new.
think The round-open printed one line I could have read past: "1 session log(s) stand in the dead-letter box and nowhere else". That is %479's stash_record guard, rostered tier lap and gated at zero, making its FIRST live catch. The threshold's dual seat says a witness must be proven able to make a sound before its silence means anything; this one was proven in a pen when it was built, and today it sounded for real.
think Behind the log stood a whole finished round -- 1,107 insertions across 14 files, stashed by a round-open 20 minutes earlier: Weave.annotate, a nine-claim witness, a module-head scan, a thirteen-phase control, a roster row, a booked red, a fold shelf. Nothing was wrong with it. It had simply never been committed, and stash@{0} is neither tree nor index, so every meter in the tree read a clean repository.
think Lindy-first and crux-first both point at the same door here, which is rare. Restoring proven work is the highest-value thing available -- the alternative is a lap re-deriving it -- and the crux inside it is not the code but the NUMBER, since the row it booked was published by a peer while it sat.
obs THE FIRST THING I CHECKED WAS WHETHER THE LANE HAD MOVED UNDER IT. git diff stash@{0}^ HEAD -- mantra/src/weave.rye came back empty, so my lane file is byte-identical at the box's base and at HEAD and the restore is a checkout rather than a merge. Three peers landed 35 files in between and not one touched mantra/. One writer per checkout held.
obs PROOF BEFORE BOOKKEEPING. I ran tools/m/mantra_weave_annotate_witness.rish before editing a single pin -- GREEN, five readings, head_missing=0, head_stale=0, ten breaks caught over thirteen phases. A restored lap is a claim until it runs on THIS tree; the card's own law is witness before narrative, and a restore is exactly where a hand is tempted to trust the record instead of the run.
obs THE RENUMBER IS THE HALF WORTH READING, AND IT HAPPENED TWICE. The box booked %485; --next answered 486 at 12:26 because a peer had published %485 (the fetcher_port row) while the box was shut, so I wrote 486 into five files and committed. The send's fetch then brought three upstream commits carrying %486 AND %487, so --next answered 488 and the row moved again on the rebase. Through both moves the row's stamp 20260906.115458 never changed. That is derived-spine.md working exactly as written: the stamp is the key and the number is a view.
obs AND THE SWEEP WAS BOUNDED BY OWNERSHIP RATHER THAN BY CARE. %484's erratum was written by a peer whose blind sed rewrote a peer's row and two upstream passages: a renumber is a rename, and a rename sweeps only what it owns. This is that rule's first outing. Five files, every one written by this seat's own lap -- the module head, the control, the runner, the box's log, the fold shelf -- listed by name into the sed rather than swept by pattern across the tree. Verified after: grep for 485 in those five reads nothing, grep for %491 reads exactly those five, and no landed row was touched.
obs AND THE SECOND SWEEP BIT A PEER, WHICH IS %492 AND THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE IS WHY IT STINGS. I passed six paths to sed on the rebase renumber: five written wholly by this seat, and session-logs/date/README-index-20260906.md, a SHARED day shelf holding two of my rows in eight. It rewrote a peer's row from %486 self-reference to %491 self-reference -- changing what their landed log says about their own red -- one hour after %484 booked that exact fault and one commit after I quoted its rule in my own commit body.
obs THE RULE WAS OBEYED AS WRITTEN, WHICH IS THE FINDING. %484 says a rename sweeps only what it owns, and every path on my list was one this seat had written into. The rule's unit is the FILE. This tree's shared pins -- REDS.md, ITINERARY.md, every README-index-*.md -- are files many hands write single lines into, so I wrote in it and I own it are different claims that read identically at a sed prompt. Ownership is per-ROW.
obs CAUGHT BY READING, NOT BY A GUARD, AND THAT IS THE SECOND FIRING. I opened the shelf to check newest-first ordering and saw a row I did not recognize; git diff xy/main -- printed the damage as one -/+ pair beside my two genuine additions. %484's erratum caught itself the same way. A lantern that fires twice becomes a loom, so the loom is named rather than built this lap: a pre-send reading that diffs every path in the round's staged set against the anointed remote and refuses a changed line the round did not mean to change. Its bound is honest -- it sees only what this round staged and can never speak for a peer's tree.
obs AND IT FIRED AGAIN ON THE NEXT REBASE, AFTER I HAD WRITTEN THE ROW. Six peers published %488, %489 and %490 while the first push was in flight, so the row renumbered a third time, to %491. That sweep's path list EXCLUDED the day shelf -- the file that had just bitten -- and still carried construction/ITINERARY.md, the most shared pin in the tree. It rewrote a peer's %488 row and its shelf link, leaving the card pointing at archive/REDS-the-doubt-published-in-the-wrong-frame-rows-491.md, a file that does not exist.
obs EXCLUDING THE FILE THAT BIT ME WAS PATTERN-MATCHING ON THE INSTANCE. The lesson is not that day shelves are shared; it is that a blanket sed is never safe on ANY shared pin, and the only safe renumber replaces exact strings the round wrote. I had the right rule written down in front of me and applied it to the one file it had already fired on. Both instances caught by the same git diff xy/main -- reading, both restored byte for byte, nothing shipped.
obs THE NUMBER MOVED THREE TIMES AND THE STAMP MOVED NONE. %485, %486, %488, %491, one renumber per rebase, roughly one every fifteen minutes, six peers publishing under it. Every one of those renumbers was mechanical and every one carried the risk that just fired twice. That is the strongest measurement I can offer for the card's own standing question: the cost of a number allocated at landing is not the bookkeeping, it is that each re-allocation is an edit across files a lap only partly owns.
obs THE CARD ENDS THIS LAP AT 40,953 OF 40,960. Seven bytes, after four condensing passes -- upstream grew the card by 499 between my two rebases, so each pass cut my own newest paragraph rather than a peer's landed one. The REDS pin refused the second row outright, 41,442 against 40,960, so it folded to its own shelf, which is what BOOKED permits and OPEN does not. Both pins sat at their ceiling on the same lap, which is a fact about the fleet's write rate rather than about either page.
obs A PUBLISHED NUMBER NEVER MOVES, AND THAT IS NOT THE SAME AS AN EARLIER STAMP WINNING. My row is stamped 20260906.115458; the %486 that kept the number is stamped 20260906.120134, 31 minutes later, and mine still moved -- because theirs had reached the anointed spine and mine had not. The law's earlier-stamp-keeps-the-number clause is for repairing a double-booking that ALREADY reached the spine, which is %470's erratum. For an unshared row the test is publication rather than priority, and reading the law the other way would have had me renumber a peer's landed row. Those two clauses point opposite ways and only publication tells them apart.
obs PROVEN BY A THIRD PARTY, TWICE. reds_ledger_monotone_witness.rish reads the spine off disk across every fold archive and the living pin, once before the rebase and once after: measured=488, disagreements=0. The gapless 1..N held across two renumbers and a fold in one lap.
obs WHY THE FOLD STAMP MOVED AND THE ROW STAMP DID NOT. The shelf header said folded on 20260906.120105 -- true of the composition, false of the fold, since the fold lands now. I moved the header to the landing stamp and wrote the recital line with the same one, so the two records of one act agree. The ROW inside keeps 20260906.115458, because that is when the red was found and a key never moves.
obs WHAT THE CARD COST, MEASURED. The pin stood at 40,091 of 40,960 -- 869 bytes of slack, and %481's account was 1,400 of them. Folding it off and writing this lap's account in its place left 40,242, so the card ends the lap with more room than it opened with. A pin at its ceiling taxes every lap a judgment call, and the cheapest way to pay that tax is to fold a closed row rather than to compress an open one.
obs GRADED WHAT I TOUCHED, AND NOTHING PUSHED A FRAME. The card A+/100 at meter on 47 resolving paths, the new fold shelf A+/100 on 2, mantra/src/weave.rye A+/100 on its comments. Three readings, three passes, no molt frame -- recorded because a grading pass that finds nothing is still the pass, and skipping it on a lap that felt like bookkeeping is how the habit goes quiet.
loom guard=mantra_weave_annotate tier=lap claims=9 readings=5 control_phases=13 breaks_caught=10 head_missing=0 head_stale=0 verdict=green
loom guard=reds_ledger_monotone measured=486,488,492 across_three_rebases headline_total=492 headline_remainder=472 disagreements=0 verdict=ok
loom restore=stash@{0} files=14 insertions=1107 lane_drift_bytes=0 renumbers=3 booked=485 landed=491 peer_lines_touched=2 peer_lines_repaired=2 rebases=2
loom pin=construction/ITINERARY.md before=40091 after=40953 bound=40960 slack=7 condense_passes=4
loom pin=construction/REDS.md row_489_on_pin=41442 bound=40960 after_fold=39475
file mantra/src/weave.rye annotate and Note restored; the head lists five operations and cites the scan that reads it.
file mantra/src/weave_annotate_witness.rye nine claims proven by doing, the fourth holding the story to the merge itself.
file tools/fixtures/m/mantra_weave_head_scan.sh the head held to the module's own pub fn declarations, both directions at zero.
file tools/fixtures/m/mantra_weave_annotate_control.sh thirteen phases -- three innocence legs and ten breaks, four over the head scan.
file tools/m/mantra_weave_annotate_witness.rish the runner; it prints the scan's lines before it asserts on them.
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri mantra_weave_annotate rostered tier lap, seated at the row's own stamp.
file construction/REDS.md headline to 488; %481 was folded off the pin by a peer while this lap worked.
file construction/archive/REDS-the-property-nobody-named-rows-491.md %491 whole, pointing at the sibling shelf a peer folded %481 to the same hour.
file construction/archive/REDS-fold-recital.md which rows folded, when, and onto which shelf.
file session-logs/date/20260906/20260906-120418_the-head-that-enumerates.kyri the box's own account, restored and renumbered, landing under its own stamp.
file construction/ITINERARY.md the account, both reds, and the loom %492 names; 40,953 of 40,960.
file construction/archive/REDS-ownership-is-per-row-rows-492.md %492 whole, folded because the pin refused it.
file construction/archive/REDS-fold-recital.md the two folds this lap made, each with its shelf and stamp.
file session-logs/date/README-index-20260906.md two rows added newest-first, and a peer's row restored byte for byte.
obs AND THE ROUND PARKED RATHER THAN LANDED, WHICH IS THE LAP'S LARGEST MEASUREMENT. Three re-integrations, three fast-forward refusals: xy took EIGHT commits from six peers between my round-open at 12:22 and 12:55. Each refusal cost a rebase, a renumber, a card condensation and a re-verify, and each rebase re-sorted the day shelf so index_row_bound red twice more -- %440's own shape, exactly as the card predicts it fires after the final rebase. The sync rota caps re-integration at three and says park; the package stands at xy refs/heads/pier/patchouli-20260906-122252, proven and whole.
obs THE PARK IS SAFE AND THE RISK IS NAMED. The card already carries two of Dream's parked packages that neither landed nor were adopted, so a park is durable storage rather than a landing. This one is one commit off b60295dce and every conflict it met was in a shared pin rather than in mantra/ -- my lane never drifted once in three hours.
obs AND THE CARD ENDED AT TWO BYTES OF SLACK. Seven condensing passes, every one cutting my own newest paragraph, because upstream grew the card roughly 700 bytes across the same three rebases. Nothing worth keeping was lost -- the full account is in this log and in the two REDS shelves -- so no cairn was marked; what a cairn would record is that the operator card is now written by eight hands into a bound sized for eight, and the arithmetic has stopped leaving room for the ninth sentence.
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- the seam is unchanged and still one word: annotate can say WHAT each side did and not yet WHERE a new line sits against the other side's delete, and the identity gap wants a wider Line; both widen a shape mantra_glow_tend_limb1 pins against Glow, so one word opens two stories. Two findings this lap want your eye. First, %492's loom: %484 and this row both reached for git diff against the anointed remote BY HAND to catch a renumber that had bitten a peer, so that reading should be an instrument in the send path rather than a habit -- agent-doable, and I did not take it because the round was already two reds deep. Second, your own standing question fired twice more here from two new sides: the ledger shows neither that a red is being WORKED nor that a lap is FINISHED BUT UNLANDED, and this lap's number moved twice in forty minutes because --next is stale the moment it is read. A claim, a seat and a stamp on an OPEN row at START would have answered all three, and eight firings in one day is the measurement rather than the argument. And two plain facts for sizing, which I think are one fact: the operator card closes at 40,958 of 40,960 after seven condensing passes, the REDS pin refused a row outright, and this round could not land in three re-integrations against eight peer commits. A bound sized per ship, a ledger numbered at landing, and a single anointed branch all price the same thing -- eight writers -- and all three came due in one round.
